# ELECTRIC OPERATION DEPARTMENT, HWH DIVISION
## Website Restructuring & Functional Redesign – Master Requirement Draft

### 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW

The existing web application is developed for the Electric Operation Department, Howrah Division, Eastern Railway.

Previously, the department had two separate operational wings:
- Diesel (DSL) Loco Operation
- Electric Loco Operation

However, due to the reduced utilization of Diesel locomotives, Diesel Loco Operation has now also come under the administrative and operational framework of Electric Operation Department.

Therefore, the existing application needs to be restructured into a centralized departmental management platform that integrates both Electric and Diesel operations.

**Primary Objective:**

To develop a single-window digital platform for all operational activities, crew management, CLI supervision, officer monitoring, training management, safety counselling, reporting, learning resources and departmental information.

The existing application should be revised wherever necessary. Already implemented features that comply with these requirements need not be redeveloped.

---

### 2. ORGANIZATIONAL HIERARCHY

The application must follow the actual organizational hierarchy of Electric Operation Department, HWH Division.

**Administrative Hierarchy:**

Sr. DEE(OP)/HWH  
↓  
AEE(OP)/HWH & AME(P)/HWH  
↓  
Chief Loco Inspectors (CLIs)  
↓  
Running Staff / Crews

**Crew Categories:**
- LPM
- LPP
- MMAN
- LPG
- LPS-I
- LPS-II
- SALP
- ALP

**Operational Control Structure:**

Under Sr. DEE(OP)/HWH, two major operational control units function:

1. TLC – Traction Loco Control (Electric)
2. PCNL – Power Control (Diesel)

Both controls operate under Sr. DEE(OP)/HWH.

TLC and PCNL should have the same operational access authority as Sr. DEE(OP), along with additional access to certain important operational tools.

---

### 3. LOBBY STRUCTURE

The application must maintain a centralized lobby management system.

**Main Lobbies under HWH Division:**
- RPH
- BWN
- HWH
- DKAE
- BHP
- TAK
- AZ
- KWAE
- PKR

**Sister Lobbies:**
- NHT
- SNT
- KURV

Each lobby will have an assigned in-charge, generally a CLI.

The application must maintain the relationship between:
- Lobby
- CLI
- Assigned Crews
- Crew Category
- Operational Jurisdiction

An administrator should be able to change the lobby assignment and CLI in-charge whenever required.

---

### 4. APPLICATION STRUCTURE – TWO MAJOR SECTIONS

The entire application should be divided into two major sections.

#### A. GLOBAL INFORMATION PORTAL

This section will be accessible without login.

It will contain departmental information and learning resources available to all users.

Proposed modules:

1. Learning Materials
   - Electric Traction
   - Diesel Traction
   - Railway Transportation Rules
   - General Rules (GR)
   - Subsidiary Rules (SR)
   - Safety Instructions
   - Technical Manuals

2. Online Quiz & Assessment
   - Topic-wise quizzes
   - Random question tests
   - Practice examinations
   - Performance analysis
   - Results and assessment history, where applicable

3. Photo Gallery
   - Divisional achievements
   - Safety campaigns
   - Training activities
   - Special events
   - Operational achievements

4. Loco Information
   - Various Electric Locomotives
   - Diesel Locomotives
   - Technical specifications
   - Important operating instructions

5. Notices & Circulars
   - Important departmental notices
   - General instructions
   - Safety circulars
   - Results and announcements

6. Important Rules & Regulations
   - GR & SR
   - Operating rules
   - Safety instructions
   - Relevant railway manuals

7. Trouble Shooting Guide
   - Electric locomotive troubleshooting
   - Diesel locomotive troubleshooting
   - Common failures
   - Emergency procedures
   - Technical guidance

8. Other Relevant Departmental Information

The administrator should be able to manage and update the contents of these sections.

#### B. LOGIN-BASED MANAGEMENT PORTAL

All operational, administrative and crew-related functions will be available through role-based login.

The proposed login hierarchy is:

**Admin → Officers → CLI/Supervisors → Crew**

TLC and PCNL will have a separate Control Login with the authority specified in this document.

The system must implement Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), ensuring that users can access only the modules and operations permitted for their roles.

---

### 5. LOGIN STRUCTURE & AUTHENTICATION

The following login credentials will be used during the initial implementation.

| User Type | Username | Initial Password |
|---|---|---|
| Admin | admin | 12345 |
| Officer – Sr. DEE(OP) | SR_DEEOP_HWH | 12345 |
| Officer – AEE(OP) | AEEOP_HWH | 12345 |
| Officer – AME(P) | AMEP_HWH | 12345 |
| CLI | Individual CLI ID | 12345 |
| Crew | Registered 10-digit mobile number | 12345 |
| TLC | TLC | 12345 |
| PCNL | PCNL | 12345 |

**Important authentication requirements:**

- Passwords must be changeable after initial login.
- Crew mobile numbers must contain exactly 10 digits.
- Country code 91 must not be included.
- Login credentials must be stored securely using password hashing.
- Admin must be able to reset user passwords.
- Duplicate mobile numbers and duplicate CLI IDs must not be permitted.
- Account activation, deactivation and password reset facilities must be available.

---

### 6. ROLE-WISE ACCESS & FUNCTIONALITY

#### 6.1 ADMIN LOGIN

Admin will have complete control over the entire application.

Admin permissions:

- Create, modify, deactivate and manage all user accounts.
- Manage officers, CLIs, crews and lobby assignments.
- Upload crew master data through Excel.
- View uploaded Excel data in an editable table.
- Add, modify and remove crew information.
- Add custom fields to the crew database whenever required.
- Manage global information and learning resources.
- Upload notices, circulars and counselling instructions.
- Manage monthly and special counselling.
- Manage training records and medical examination records.
- Manage Google Sheets links.
- Manage Unusual Incident Tracker directory.
- Access all reports and operational dashboards.
- Access all Important Tools.
- Configure application settings and permissions.
- View all historical records.

Admin will have unrestricted access to the application.

#### 6.2 OFFICER LOGIN

Officers include:

- Sr. DEE(OP)/HWH
- AEE(OP)/HWH
- AME(P)/HWH

Officers will primarily have monitoring, reviewing and reporting authority.

**Officer Dashboard:**

1. Overall Crew Overview
2. CLI-wise Crew Details
3. Lobby-wise Crew Details
4. Daily CLI Movement
5. CLI Performance Monitoring
6. Crew Performance Monitoring
7. Crew Counselling Status
8. Training Due Reports
9. Medical Examination Due Reports
10. Notice Acknowledgement Status
11. Various Filtered Reports
12. Unusual Incident Tracker

Officers must be able to filter data by:

- Date
- CLI
- Lobby
- Crew Name
- Crew ID
- Crew Category
- Training Category
- Performance Parameters
- Other available crew information

**Officer Permissions:**

- View all crew and CLI information.
- View CLI-wise and lobby-wise reports.
- Monitor daily CLI activities and movements.
- Generate filtered reports.
- Add remarks to CLI performance.
- Add remarks to crew performance.
- Review counselling and acknowledgement status.
- Review training and medical examination compliance.
- Access Unusual Incident Tracker.

Officers must not be permitted to directly edit master crew information or CLI-entered operational records.

They can only add remarks and observations in designated fields.

#### 6.3 TLC / PCNL CONTROL LOGIN

TLC and PCNL will have the same operational viewing and monitoring authority as Sr. DEE(OP).

However, they will have additional access to important operational tools.

Control-specific facilities:

- View all crews and CLIs.
- View CLI-wise and lobby-wise crew information.
- Monitor operational performance.
- Access relevant operational reports.
- Access Unusual Incident Tracker.
- Access Important Tools.
- Monitor relevant crew-related activities.

The distinction between Officer and Control permissions must be maintained through RBAC.

#### 6.4 CLI LOGIN

CLI is the primary supervisory authority responsible for the crews assigned to them.

Each CLI will have an individual dashboard.

**CLI Dashboard:**

On login, the CLI should immediately see:

- Assigned lobby/lobbies.
- Total assigned crews.
- Category-wise crew count.
- Crew-wise personal information.
- Official information.
- Training and medical details.
- Counselling records.
- Current problems and observations.
- Crew performance records.
- Previous counselling history.

**CLI Functionalities:**

1. View all crews assigned to the CLI.
2. Add and update crew-related information.
3. Record crew counselling.
4. Record monthly counselling.
5. Record special counselling.
6. Record footplate inspection and observations.
7. Record CVVRS inspection.
8. Record SPM analysis and observations.
9. Record crew performance.
10. Record operational deficiencies and irregularities.
11. Record current crew-related problems.
12. Record follow-up observations.
13. Record unusual operational incidents.
14. View acknowledgement status of notices and counselling.

CLI-entered records must be available to officers for monitoring and report generation.

**CLI Assignment Restriction:**

A CLI should normally be permitted to edit only the crew records assigned to them.

Admin may reassign crews or change CLI responsibilities.

#### 6.5 CREW LOGIN

Every crew member will have an individual dashboard.

The dashboard should contain:

**Personal Information**
- Name
- Employee ID
- Mobile Number
- Designation
- Crew Category
- Lobby
- Assigned CLI
- Other personal information

**Official Information**
- Official designation
- Posting information
- Relevant operational details
- Other service-related information

**Training Information**
- Training history
- Completed training
- Upcoming training
- Due dates

**Medical Information**
- PME details
- Last medical examination
- Next due date

**Counselling Section**
- Monthly counselling
- Special counselling
- Other applicable instructions

Crew members must be able to read and acknowledge notices and counselling issued to them.

Crew members must not be permitted to modify their official master records.

---

### 7. CLI MOVEMENT MONITORING

A dedicated module should be developed for monitoring daily CLI movement and activities.

CLIs perform various supervisory activities throughout the division, including:

- Footplate inspections with crews.
- CVVRS checking.
- SPM analysis.
- Crew counselling.
- Loco-related failure attendance.
- Crew-related problem attendance.
- Safety inspections.
- Other supervisory duties.

The system should allow CLIs to enter their daily activities and movement details.

Officers should be able to monitor:

- Daily CLI movement.
- Date-wise activities.
- Location-wise movement.
- Crew attended.
- Activity performed.
- Remarks and observations.
- CLI-wise performance.

**Data Retention Policy:**

CLI movement and daily activity records should be retained for three months only.

A scheduled daily CRON job should automatically remove records older than the permitted retention period.

Important: This three-month retention policy applies only to CLI movement/activity records. Crew master data, counselling history, training records, medical records and other permanent departmental records must not be deleted under this policy.

---

### 8. CREW MASTER DATABASE & EXCEL MANAGEMENT

Admin will maintain the master crew database through Excel uploads.

**Initial Upload:**

Admin will upload an Excel file containing:

- CLI ID
- CLI Name
- CLI Mobile Number
- Crew Name
- Crew Employee ID
- Crew Mobile Number
- Crew Designation
- Crew Category
- Lobby
- Other available crew information

The exact columns will be finalized after receiving the Excel master file.

**Excel Upload Workflow:**

1. Admin uploads an Excel file.
2. System reads and processes the Excel data.
3. Uploaded information is displayed in a table view.
4. Admin can inspect the uploaded data before saving.
5. Admin can edit any field directly in the table.
6. Admin can add or remove records.
7. System validates duplicate IDs and mobile numbers.
8. Admin confirms and saves the data.
9. The updated information becomes available in the respective dashboards.

**Dynamic Field Management:**

Admin should be able to add new custom fields to the crew database whenever the department requires additional information.

For example, if a new reporting requirement arises, Admin should be able to create a new field without requiring major redevelopment of the application.

The system should support Excel export and future Excel-based updates.

---

### 9. TRAINING & MEDICAL MANAGEMENT MODULE

A dedicated Training and Medical Management System must be developed.

Every crew member undergoes various types of training during their service.

**A. Training Categories**

1. DSL Traction Training
2. Electric Traction Training
3. Railway Transportation Rules Training (GR & SR)

**B. Training Lifecycle**

At the time of initial appointment/joining, the crew must undergo the prescribed initial training.

After completion of initial training, refresher training is required at prescribed intervals, generally every three years, until the next applicable promotion or training event.

**C. Promotional Training**

The system must maintain promotional training records.

Examples:

- SALP to LPS: Promotional Training.
- SALP to LPG: Promotional Training.
- LPG to LPP: Promotional Training.
- LPG to MMAN: Promotional Training.

The actual applicable training requirement will be configurable as per departmental rules.

After promotion, the system must maintain the subsequent training cycle according to the applicable training rules.

**D. Training Data Fields**

For each crew member, maintain:

- Training Category
- Training Type
- Last Training Completed Date
- Next Training Due Date
- Training Status
- Training History
- Remarks

**E. Weekly Excel Update**

Admin will upload an Excel file every week containing updated training information.

The application should:

1. Import the Excel data.
2. Match records using Employee ID.
3. Display the imported records in table format.
4. Allow Admin to inspect and edit the records.
5. Update the corresponding training information.
6. Maintain the historical record of completed training.
7. Recalculate the next due date wherever applicable.

Admin should also be able to manually update completed and due dates.

**F. PME / Medical Examination**

Medical examination requirements:

- Up to 45 years of age: Every three years.
- Above 45 years of age: Every year.

The system should calculate the next medical due date based on the last completed medical examination and the applicable age-based requirement.

**G. Officer Training Dashboard**

Officers should have access to training compliance reports.

Reports should include:

- Training completed.
- Training due.
- Overdue training.
- Category-wise training status.
- Lobby-wise training status.
- CLI-wise training status.
- Crew-wise training history.
- Medical examination due.
- Medical examination overdue.

Detailed training rules and exceptions will be incorporated in a later phase after the relevant departmental instructions are provided.

---

### 10. NOTICE & COUNSELLING MANAGEMENT

The existing Daily Safety Counselling option should be removed as a separate dashboard/module.

Counselling will instead be available within the respective user login dashboards.

**A. Notice Management**

Officers and authorized Admin users should be able to issue notices.

Notice recipients may be:

- All Crews.
- All CLIs.
- Both Crews and CLIs.
- Selected Lobbies.
- Selected individual users.

Every recipient must read and acknowledge the notice.

**B. Monthly Counselling**

Officers should be able to issue monthly counselling.

The system should maintain:

- Counselling title.
- Counselling content.
- Issue date.
- Target recipients.
- Acknowledgement status.
- Pending acknowledgement list.

**C. Special Counselling**

Officers should be able to issue special counselling whenever necessary.

It should support selected crews, selected CLIs, individual lobbies or the entire division.

**D. Acknowledgement System**

Every notice and counselling record should have:

- Read status.
- Acknowledged status.
- Acknowledgement date and time.
- Pending list.
- User-wise acknowledgement history.

Acknowledgement records must be retained for future verification.

---

